  m_Name: DieselEngineData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Diesel Engine
  engineDescription: A Diesel Engine is an internal combustion engine that converts
    the chemical energy of diesel fuel into mechanical power through compression
    ignition. Its main components include the cylinder block, cylinder head, piston,
    connecting rod, crankshaft, fuel injector, intake and exhaust valves, camshaft,
    combustion chamber, lubrication system, and cooling system. During operation,
    air is drawn into the cylinder during the intake stroke and is then highly compressed
    by the piston during the compression stroke. The high compression ratio raises
    the temperature of the air sufficiently for ignition. Diesel fuel is injected
    into the hot compressed air near the end of the compression stroke, causing the
    fuel to ignite without the need for a spark plug. The resulting combustion produces
    high-pressure gases that force the piston downward during the power stroke. The
    crankshaft converts the reciprocating piston motion into rotary mechanical output.
    Diesel engines are widely used in automobiles, trucks, generators, agricultural
    machinery, marine vessels, construction equipment, and industrial power systems.
    From an engineering perspective, understanding compression ratio, fuel injection,
    combustion, thermal efficiency, air-fuel ratio, cooling, lubrication, emissions,
    turbocharging, and engine wear is essential for achieving efficient and reliable
    engine performance.
  engineCategory: Engineering
